From: TaoWangRUB <37864846+TaoWangRUB@users.noreply.github.com>
Subject: [PATCH] Multicamera: stop orphaning camera 0's stereo partner

Every non-RGBD / non-Multisensor run declares camera 0 a "depth camera" with
allow_stereo_track_for_depth = false. FrustumIntersectionGraph then

  1. erases every secondary edge that LANDS on camera 0,
  2. deletes any primary left with an empty secondary list
     ("remove primary cameras that have no stereo pairs"),
  3. re-adds camera 0 itself - but nothing re-adds its orphaned partner.

MultiSOFGPU builds mono_sof_ from primary_cameras(), so that partner never gets a
mono tracker created: it is never tracked and produces no observations, in any
mode. Precision does not help - the pruning runs after mode selection.

A surround rig of DISJOINT pairs is maximally exposed, because every virtual
camera has exactly one partner, so camera 0's partner is always the casualty.

Measured on the BEV 4x IMX296 fisheye rig (8 virtual pinholes, 4 disjoint pairs),
run6 replay, features per frame:

  before   cam2+45  27.6 mean, 845 of 1299 frames with ZERO
           other 7  302-362 mean, 0 frames with zero
  after    cam2+45  325.9 mean, 0 frames with zero
           all 8    326-356 mean, 0 frames with zero

Confirmed by reordering the camera list: the dead camera moves to the new
camera 0's partner exactly as this mechanism predicts, and the previously dead
one recovers fully. scripts/vo/verify_fig_primaries.sh runs cuVSLAM's own
selection logic standalone (no GPU, no images) and gates on it.

Keeping the edges costs nothing in Multicamera mode - there is no depth camera -
and camera 0 remains guaranteed-primary either way.

--- a/libs/cuvslam/cuvslam2.cpp
+++ b/libs/cuvslam/cuvslam2.cpp
@@
   } else {
     depth_ids.push_back(static_cast<CameraId>(0));
+    enable_depth_stereo_tracking_fig = true;
   }
